      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "What is in architecture_plan_final.pdf?",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-23T14:37:57.621401"
      },
      {
        "role": "ai",
        "content": "The PDF titled \"architecture_plan_final.pdf\" contains information about a Next.js discriminative code review model. It includes details on:\n\n1. Architecture Planning \u2014 Next.js Discriminative Code Review Model, pages 5 and 6.\n2. Evaluation framework with three pillars and four baselines, page 10.\n3. Project timeline across nine phases over approximately 19 weeks, page 4.\n\nThe document discusses the architecture of a model that uses LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) to fine-tune pre-trained models on specific tasks. It mentions parameters such as rank r=8 and hidden dimension d=768, with each layer contributing 2 x 768 x 8 = 12,288 parameters per layer. The model reduces the total LoRA overhead from base model size by applying it to Q and V weight matrices in the top four layers, resulting in a low initialisation of matrix B that ensures fine-tuning starts exactly at the pre-trained checkpoint with no perturbation.\n\nThe document also explains how multi-task learning helps improve performance on individual heads by forcing the shared encoder to build representations that capture correlations between issue category, severity, and quality signals. It provides details on dataset construction (GitHub PR collection) and evaluation metrics for different head types: category, quality regression, severity, and line-level classification.\n\nThe project timeline outlines nine phases across approximately 19 weeks, including stages such as literature review, data collection pipeline, baseline fine-tuning, ablation study, human evaluation, ONNX quantisation, deployment tooling, and paper writing.",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-23T14:37:57.621410"
      }
    ],
    "last_message_at": "2026-03-23T14:37:57.621411"
