    "messages": [
      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "What information is in architecture_plan_final.pdf?",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-23T13:11:56.704525"
      },
      {
        "role": "ai",
        "content": "The PDF contains detailed information about a Next.js discriminative code review model's architecture plan. Key points include:\n\n1. Architecture Overview: The document describes how the system works from data collection to deployment, including pre-trained transformer encoder fine-tuning and local ONNX quantization.\n\n2. Model Parameters:\n   - Each LoRA adapter contributes 2 x 768 x 8 = 12,288 parameters per layer.\n   - Applied to Q and V weight matrices in the top four layers, the total LoRA overhead is 196,608 parameters.\n\n3. Model Training:\n   - The model uses rank r=8 and hidden dimension d=768 for each LoRA adapter.\n   - Zero initialisation of matrix B ensures fine-tuning starts exactly at pre-trained checkpoint without perturbation.\n\n4. Multi-Task Output Heads:\n   - Four simultaneous outputs from one shared encoder, with three heads using [CLS] aggregate vector; one head uses all token vectors.\n   - All heads are single linear projections followed by an activation \u2014 shallow design for fast inference.\n\n5. Dataset Construction:\n   - GitHub PR collection and filtering, label extraction, train/val/test split.\n\n6. Evaluation Framework:\n   - Three pillars: automatic metrics, human evaluation, ablation studies.\n   - Metrics include F1 scores, MSE, accuracy, etc., with specific heads evaluated separately.\n\n7. Project Timeline:\n   - Nine phases across approximately 19 weeks, including literature review, data collection pipeline, baseline model training, LoRA fine-tuning and hyperparameter grid search, ablation study, human evaluation, ONNX quantization, and paper writing.\n\nThe PDF provides comprehensive details on the architecture, parameters, training process, dataset construction, evaluation framework, project timeline, and overall system overview.",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-23T13:11:56.704555"
      }
    ],
    "last_message_at": "2026-03-23T13:11:56.704557"
